course: dictionary
lesson: 2264
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them tell you which meaning the writer wants. The word crease is a good example, because it can name a thing or describe an action.
explain: A crease is a line or fold made when paper, cloth or skin is pressed or crumpled. To crease means to make that fold, or to become creased. «Origami is the Japanese-rooted art of folding paper into shapes; sharp creases and corners lined up exactly make the model neat.» Here crease names the fold itself.
explain: Crease also has a sports meaning. In cricket, the crease is the white line where the batter stands, not a fold in anything. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the sentence? Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A crease is a line or fold made when paper, cloth or skin is pressed or crumpled.
fact: To crease means to make a fold or line in something, or to become creased.
fact: In cricket, the crease is the white line where the batter stands.
fact: Context clues such as paper, folding or batter tell you which meaning of a word is meant.
exercise: «Cardboard is made by running paper through hot ridged rollers... then cut and creased into boxes.» What does creased mean here? | Painted in bright colours | *Folded or marked with lines so it can bend into a box | Stuck flat onto a wall | Cut into tiny pieces
exercise: «Each material has its own rules: paper needs sharp creases.» What is a crease in this sentence? | A type of cloth | A sharp pair of scissors | A kind of glue | *A fold or line made in paper
exercise: In cricket, what is the crease? | *The white line where the batter stands | A pile of dirty clothes | A wrinkle on a face | A fold in a shirt
test: «Origami ... sharp creases and corners lined up exactly make the model neat.» In this sentence, what kind of word is crease? | A verb, an action: to fold | A colour | *A noun, a thing: a fold | A question word
test: Ella irons her shirt to iron out the creases. What is she removing? | The colour of the cloth | The buttons on the shirt | *The folds and lines in the cloth | The sleeves of the shirt
exam: Which sentence uses crease to mean a fold in cloth? | The crease was the white line at the end of the pitch. | He wanted to crease the field, not the paper. | The batter ran to the crease before the ball arrived. | *She ironed a sharp crease down the front of her trousers.
